# ABP Framework — Windsurf Rules

## Auto-activation (this file is always on)

Activate for the whole task — not only scaffolding requests — as soon as **any** ABP signal is
present: `Volo.Abp*` package references, `AbpModule` subclasses, `AbpDbContext`, `AggregateRoot` /
`FullAuditedEntity` / `IRepository<,>`, `AbpController` / `AbpPageModel`, `appsettings` with `"Abp"`
/ `"AuthServer"` / `"RemoteServices"`, projects ending in `.Domain.Shared` / `.Application.Contracts`
/ `.HttpApi` / `.DbMigrator` / `.EntityFrameworkCore`, or `abp.json` / `.abp/`.

Say one line when it activates — `ABP project detected (signal: …) — running Phase 0 before writing
code` — then run Phase 0 below. If no signal matches, do not inject ABP patterns. The authoritative
signal list is `abp-dev/references/context-intake.md` §0.0.

You are an expert ABP Framework developer. Always follow ABP conventions precisely.
Server-side stack: **MVC or Razor Pages + EF Core** — but always detect the real UI stack from the
project before generating code (see "Detect the UI stack" below). Angular and MAUI have their own
reference files; do not emit Razor syntax into an `angular/` project or vice versa.

## How to answer ABP questions

0. **Phase 0 — context intake (mandatory, `abp-dev/references/context-intake.md`), triggered by the
   auto-activation rule above.** Before writing
   any code: inspect the existing code through a knowledge graph (`@Codebase`, Miro/RepoWiki or graph
   MCP if available, plain search as fallback) and record namespace, ABP version, layers, UI stack,
   theme, and the entities/services/permissions/DbSets that already exist; read the repo's plan and
   design `.md` files (`docs/`, `plans/`, `specs/`, `DESIGN.md`, ADRs) and honour them; for UI work
   ask the user whether the **Stitch MCP** is connected (real screens + design tokens), otherwise
   take the **project's existing UI as the reference** and reuse its theme, components and JS
   patterns; then show a reuse / extend / add summary with open questions and confirm scope before
   generating. Finish the phase with the SOLID + ABP design-pattern check in that file.
1. Read **`abp-dev/references/INDEX.md`** — it is the single routing table that maps every topic to
   its reference file and its live-verified documentation URL. Do not rely on a remembered list of
   reference files — the index is what makes every file reachable.
2. Read the reference file(s) INDEX.md maps the question to.
3. Fetch the official documentation URL from the same row to get current API details.
4. Synthesise all sources before generating code or advice.

If a fetch fails (network unavailable), rely on the reference file and note the answer is based on cached documentation.

## Detect the UI stack

| Signal in the target project | Stack | Reference file |
|---|---|---|
| `Pages/**/*.cshtml` | Razor Pages | `abp-dev/references/ui-razorpages.md` |
| `Controllers/` + `Views/` | ASP.NET Core MVC | `abp-dev/references/ui-mvc.md` |
| `angular/package.json` | Angular SPA | `abp-dev/references/ui-angular.md` |
| `*.Maui/` project | .NET MAUI | `abp-dev/references/maui.md` |
| `React Native` folder / Expo config | React Native | `abp-dev/references/react-native.md` |

## Core ABP Principles (always apply)

1. Layered architecture: Domain → Application → Infrastructure (EF Core) → UI (MVC / Razor Pages / Angular / MAUI)
2. Never expose entities to the presentation layer — always use DTOs
3. Business logic lives in entities and domain services, not in application services
4. Application services orchestrate — call domain services/repositories and map to DTOs
5. Protected/private setters on entity properties — enforce changes through methods
6. Always use IGuidGenerator to generate Guid IDs — never call Guid.NewGuid() directly
7. Auto-register services — ABP discovers ITransientDependency, ISingletonDependency, IScopedDependency automatically
8. ConfigureByConvention() must always be called in EF Core model configuration
9. Permissions are defined in Application.Contracts, then checked in application services and in whichever UI the project uses (Razor Pages / MVC views, Angular requiredPolicy, MAUI view models)
10. Reuse before adding — Phase 0 tells you what already exists; extend an existing entity, domain service, permission group or UI pattern instead of creating a parallel second one, and keep SOLID (one aggregate per consistency boundary, depend on domain-declared abstractions, branch with events/DI strategies/interceptors instead of type checks)

## Typical Layered Solution Structure

Acme.MyApp/
├── Acme.MyApp.Domain.Shared/         ← Enums, consts, DTOs shared with clients
├── Acme.MyApp.Domain/                ← Entities, domain services, repo interfaces
├── Acme.MyApp.Application.Contracts/ ← App service interfaces, DTOs, permissions
├── Acme.MyApp.Application/           ← App service implementations
├── Acme.MyApp.EntityFrameworkCore/   ← DbContext, EF Core repo implementations
├── Acme.MyApp.DbMigrator/            ← CLI tool: migrate + seed
├── Acme.MyApp.HttpApi/               ← (optional) manual API controllers
├── Acme.MyApp.Web/                   ← MVC or Razor Pages UI (Views/ vs Pages/)
└── angular/                          ← replaces *.Web with -u angular; own package.json, HTTP-only access
